            <h2>Image Dimensions vs. File Size vs. Megapixels: The Critical Difference</h2>
            <p>
              Content creators and web developers frequently confuse <strong>pixel dimensions</strong> with <strong>file size</strong>. While they are related, they measure fundamentally different properties:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Image Dimensions (px):</strong> The physical grid of pixels defining image resolution (e.g., <code>1920 × 1080</code>). Dimensions dictate how sharp and clear the image displays on screens of various sizes.</li>
              <li><strong>File Size (KB / MB):</strong> The amount of binary byte storage occupied on your hard drive, smartphone flash memory, or web hosting server (e.g., <code>1.4 MB</code> or <code>280 KB</code>). File size is governed by file formats and compression algorithms.</li>
              <li><strong>Megapixels (MP):</strong> The aggregate number of millions of pixels contained within the image. A 12 MP smartphone sensor typically captures photos measuring approximately <code>4032 × 3024 px</code>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Crucially, two images can share the <em>exact same 1920 × 1080 pixel dimensions</em> while having vastly different file sizes. An uncompressed PNG graphic might consume <code>3.5 MB</code>, while the same graphic saved as an optimized WebP or AVIF file might require only <code>180 KB</code> without any perceptible loss in visual clarity.
            </p>

            <h2>Standard Social Media &amp; Web Image Dimensions Cheat Sheet (2026 Reference)</h2>
            <p>
              Publishing images with incorrect pixel dimensions forces social media platforms to recompress and crop your files automatically, often introducing severe artifacts and blurriness. Follow these standardized dimensions for optimal presentation:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>YouTube:</strong> Video Thumbnails (<code>1280 × 720 px</code>, 16:9, max 2 MB), Channel Banner (<code>2560 × 1440 px</code>), Profile Picture (<code>800 × 800 px</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>Instagram:</strong> Square Feed Post (<code>1080 × 1080 px</code>, 1:1), Vertical Feed Post (<code>1080 × 1350 px</code>, 4:5), Stories &amp; Reels (<code>1080 × 1920 px</code>, 9:16), Profile Photo (<code>320 × 320 px</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>TikTok:</strong> Full Vertical Video &amp; Carousel Cover (<code>1080 × 1920 px</code>, 9:16), Profile Picture (<code>200 × 200 px</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>X (formerly Twitter):</strong> Timeline In-Stream Image (<code>1200 × 675 px</code>, 16:9), Header Banner (<code>1500 × 500 px</code>, 3:1), Profile Avatar (<code>400 × 400 px</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>LinkedIn:</strong> Feed Post Image (<code>1200 × 627 px</code> or <code>1080 × 1080 px</code>), Company Cover Banner (<code>1128 × 191 px</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>Website &amp; SEO:</strong> Open Graph Social Share Image (<code>1200 × 630 px</code>, 1.91:1), Full-Width Hero Banner (<code>1920 × 1080 px</code> or <code>2560 × 1440 px</code>).</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Why Accurate Image Dimensions Matter for Web Performance, Core Web Vitals, and SEO</h2>
            <p>
              Images account for more than 50% of the total page byte weight on modern websites. Uploading raw, uninspected camera files directly to a website causes significant technical SEO problems:
            </p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Inflated Largest Contentful Paint (LCP):</strong> If your hero banner image measures <code>5000 × 3300 px</code> when it is only displayed at <code>800 px</code> wide on mobile screens, mobile visitors waste cellular data downloading megabytes of unused pixels, directly delaying FCP and LCP.</li>
              <li><strong>Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS):</strong> When browsers parse an HTML document, they cannot determine an image's layout footprint until the image file headers finish downloading unless you specify explicit <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, or CSS <code>aspect-ratio</code> attributes. Missing dimensions cause page content to jump jarringly, triggering poor CLS scores.</li>
              <li><strong>Responsive <code>srcset</code> Configuration:</strong> Checking the native dimensions of your source photography allows developers to generate accurately scaled image variants (e.g., 360w, 720w, 1080w, 1920w) using modern responsive HTML markup.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Supported Digital Image Formats: PNG, JPG, WebP, AVIF, GIF, and SVG</h2>
            <p>
              Our <strong>image dimensions checker</strong> evaluates all widely used web and graphic design formats:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>JPEG / JPG:</strong> The universal standard for digital photography, utilizing lossy compression to keep photographic file sizes manageable.</li>
              <li><strong>PNG:</strong> Lossless format ideal for screenshots, UI graphics, illustrations, and images requiring alpha-channel transparency.</li>
              <li><strong>WebP:</strong> Modern web format developed by Google offering superior lossy and lossless compression with transparency and animation support, typically 25–35% smaller than JPEG.</li>
              <li><strong>AVIF:</strong> Next-generation image format based on the AV1 video codec, providing industry-leading compression efficiency and HDR color gamut support.</li>
              <li><strong>GIF:</strong> Legacy bitmap format supporting 256 indexed colors and looping animations.</li>
              <li><strong>SVG:</strong> Scalable Vector Graphics defined by XML markup. Unlike raster graphics, SVGs scale infinitely to any resolution without pixelation, defined by an intrinsic <code>viewBox</code> coordinate system.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Complete Client-Side Privacy: Why Browser-Based Inspection Is Safer</h2>
            <p>
              Many online file inspection websites require you to upload your files to remote cloud servers, exposing sensitive business slides, personal family photos, or unreleased product screenshots to remote server logs and third-party storage buckets.
            </p>
            <p>
              Our <strong>Image Dimensions Checker</strong> runs 100% locally in your web browser sandbox using modern JavaScript HTML5 File and Blob APIs. When you drop an image, your browser creates a temporary memory pointer (<code>blob:</code> URI), decodes the pixel geometry directly on your device's CPU/GPU, and immediately releases the allocated memory upon reset. Not a single byte of your image ever leaves your computer or phone.
            </p>

